window.PORTFOLIO_PROJECTS = {"order":["print-orchestrator","pfc-supervisor","ai-server","e3ng","voron-systems","metuned","motorsports","espresso-machine","uei-lab","abb-rexroth","makerspace-manufacturing"],"projects":{"print-orchestrator":{"id":"print-orchestrator","tone":"blue","eyebrow":"Printer fleet management","title":"Print Orchestrator","lede":"A shared operating surface for a mixed printer fleet. I built it to make printer state easier to understand, reduce interface hopping, and keep recovery tools close to the evidence that explains the problem.","status":["Working system","PrusaLink + Moonraker","Local network","Active development"],"image":"assets/screenshots/print-orchestrator-dashboard.png","imageAlt":"Print Orchestrator fleet dashboard showing a Prusa Core One, Alpine, and E3NG printer cards","imageCaption":"Real dashboard capture supplied from the working Print Orchestrator interface.","metrics":[{"value":"3","label":"printers configured in the captured fleet"},{"value":"2","label":"printer API families brought together"},{"value":"1","label":"operator view for cameras, jobs, power, and health"},{"value":"Human-first","label":"status and recovery language"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>Why I built one dashboard for a mixed printer fleet.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>My printers did not share one control system. One used PrusaLink while the others used Moonraker and Klipper. That meant separate browser tabs, different status terms, and no quick way to see whether a problem came from the printer, camera, power plug, job, or detection service.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">System</p><h3>What I built</h3><p>I built a fleet dashboard that keeps each machine honest about its own capabilities while still giving the operator one place to work. The cards show connectivity, camera state, part detection, power mapping, temperatures, active jobs, files, and printer-specific controls.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Ownership</p><h3>My role</h3><p>I own the architecture, interface design, service integration, release packaging, testing, and ongoing recovery work. The project is built around my actual printer fleet, so every screen is shaped by real machine states rather than a generic dashboard template.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Constraint</p><h3>The constraint that mattered</h3><p>The biggest design constraint was not visual polish; it was avoiding false confidence. A printer can be online while its camera is stale, its power plug is unreachable, or part detection is not configured. Those states need to stay separate so an operator knows what is safe to do.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>The interface had to respect how different each machine really is.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Keep device differences visible</h3><p>I normalize the overall fleet view, but I do not pretend a PrusaLink printer and a Moonraker/Klipper printer expose the same controls. Cards render the features that are actually available.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Separate machine state from supporting services</h3><p>Connectivity, job state, camera state, power, and detection are shown independently. This makes partial failures understandable and avoids turning every issue into a generic &ldquo;offline&rdquo; banner.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Write recovery states for an operator</h3><p>The interface says what failed, what remains available, and what action is reasonable next. That is more useful than a raw exception or an unexplained red light.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Treat dangerous actions differently</h3><p>Power-off, emergency-stop, firmware, and console actions use stronger visual language and are placed close to the machine context that justifies them.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"workflow","label":"Workflow","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Workflow</p><h2>What happens between a printer update and an operator action.</h2></div><p>A step-by-step view of the data and operator workflow.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-flow\"> <article><span>01</span><h3>Collect</h3><p>Pull each machine&rsquo;s native status, temperatures, camera, job, and power information.</p></article>\n<article><span>02</span><h3>Normalize</h3><p>Map shared concepts without hiding printer-specific capabilities or unsupported features.</p></article>\n<article><span>03</span><h3>Present</h3><p>Show the operator the machine, supporting services, and available actions together.</p></article>\n<article><span>04</span><h3>Recover</h3><p>Record the failure, guide the next action, and verify that the machine returned to a safe state.</p></article></div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Screenshots and build evidence from the working system.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Fleet overview with configured, connected, printing, paused, and offline counts</li><li>Per-printer camera, temperature, job, power, part-detection, files, and console cards</li><li>PrusaLink and Moonraker/Klipper integration in one visual system</li><li>Notification, Hermes, server, power, and administration navigation</li><li>Responsive card layout and operator-specific layout persistence</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure class=\"screenshot\"><button data-caption=\"Full fleet control surface &mdash; The live capture shows the real density of the dashboard: summary state, filters, camera evidence, independent service cards, power controls, and printer consoles.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Full fleet control surface\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/screenshots/print-orchestrator-dashboard.png\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Full fleet control surface</strong>The live capture shows the real density of the dashboard: summary state, filters, camera evidence, independent service cards, power controls, and printer consoles.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Machine behind the interface &mdash; The software is tied to the physical printer fleet rather than being a disconnected UI exercise.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Machine behind the interface\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/e3ng-img-7200-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Machine behind the interface</strong>The software is tied to the physical printer fleet rather than being a disconnected UI exercise.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Electronics and networked toolhead work &mdash; Controller, Raspberry Pi, and CAN-style integration are part of the machine context the dashboard has to represent.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Electronics and networked toolhead work\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/e3ng-img-7128-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Electronics and networked toolhead work</strong>Controller, Raspberry Pi, and CAN-style integration are part of the machine context the dashboard has to represent.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A clearer way to operate, troubleshoot, and extend the fleet.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Operational clarity</h3><p>The fleet can be understood from one screen without erasing the differences between machines.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Faster fault isolation</h3><p>An operator can see whether the issue is the printer, camera, power plug, detection service, or job state before taking action.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>A platform to extend</h3><p>The same structure can support job routing, richer part detection, maintenance history, and tighter PFC/Hermes coordination.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The next work is focused on improving part-detection feedback, making recovery history easier to compare, and turning more repeated operator actions into safe, testable workflows.","fallbackPage":"projects/print-orchestrator/"},"pfc-supervisor":{"id":"pfc-supervisor","tone":"green","eyebrow":"Server power management","title":"PFC Supervisor","lede":"A control plane that lets the main compute server sleep through long idle windows while a low-power Pi keeps schedules, wake protection, status, and recovery logic available.","status":["Working system","Power-state orchestration","Hermes schedule cache","Measured + estimated ledger"],"image":"assets/screenshots/pfc-power-management-top.png","imageAlt":"PFC Supervisor power management dashboard showing current load, observed sleep, and avoided energy","imageCaption":"Real PFC power-management capture. Savings are shown as the dashboard currently estimates them from retained history and configured baselines.","metrics":[{"value":"13.19 kWh","label":"current seven-day avoided-energy estimate"},{"value":"95.0 h","label":"observed sleep retained in history"},{"value":"68.23 kWh","label":"current projected 30-day savings"},{"value":"$290.54","label":"current projected annual value"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>The AI server did not need to stay awake when no work was running.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>The B70 compute server is useful when AI, Minecraft, print-control, or scheduled jobs need it, but leaving the full system awake all day wastes power. A basic timer was not enough because the work is irregular and some jobs must wake the server before they run.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">System</p><h3>What I built</h3><p>PFC splits responsibility between the main server and a low-power Pi. The Pi keeps the control surface, Hermes schedule cache, wake protections, policy checks, and retained history available even when the main server is asleep.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Ownership</p><h3>My role</h3><p>I designed the operating model, dashboard, power policy, runtime reconciliation, schedule-cache visibility, eligibility matrix, release process, and recovery behavior. The project is still evolving as I add better attribution, notifications, and cross-system checks.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Safeguard</p><h3>The safety rule</h3><p>Automatic suspend fails safe. If telemetry is stale, a protected job is near, an inhibition lease is active, a workload is unknown, or a required runtime state cannot be verified, PFC blocks sleep and explains why.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Automatic sleep only works when the safety logic is visible.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Keep the Pi awake, not the whole server</h3><p>The low-power controller retains the minimum services needed for schedules, wake dispatch, status, and operator access while the high-power node sleeps.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Make every sleep decision inspectable</h3><p>The eligibility matrix shows each rule as eligible, blocked, unknown, or stale, plus the subsystem that supplied the decision.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Protect scheduled work before it starts</h3><p>Hermes jobs are cached with a wake-dispatch window and protected-until window so the server can wake before execution instead of after a job is already late.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Separate estimates from direct measurement</h3><p>The energy ledger labels baseline-based estimates and keeps coverage visible. A direct meter source is the next step for stronger attribution.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"savings","label":"Power savings","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Power savings</p><h2>The current seven-day snapshot shows 95 hours of sleep.</h2></div><p>Observed sleep is separated from estimated energy savings so the result remains honest.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-savings-spotlight\">\n<div class=\"project-savings-main\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Seven-day ledger</p><h3>13.19 kWh avoided</h3><p>The same snapshot reports 95.0 hours of observed sleep, an average of 2.27 kWh saved per day, 68.23 kWh projected over 30 days, and a projected annual value of $290.54.</p><div aria-label=\"Illustrative seven-day avoided energy chart\" class=\"project-savings-chart\"><article><i style=\"--height:90%\"></i><span>Fri</span></article><article><i style=\"--height:94%\"></i><span>Sat</span></article><article><i style=\"--height:87%\"></i><span>Sun</span></article><article><i style=\"--height:55%\"></i><span>Mon</span></article><article><i style=\"--height:63%\"></i><span>Tue</span></article><article><i style=\"--height:52%\"></i><span>Wed</span></article><article><i style=\"--height:18%\"></i><span>Thu</span></article></div></div>\n<aside class=\"project-savings-note\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What the number means</p><strong>Useful estimate, visible caveat</strong><p>PFC currently attributes most host energy from configured wattage baselines and observed state history. The dashboard deliberately keeps that limitation next to the savings claim.</p><ul><li>Observed sleep is retained from real state history.</li><li>Avoided energy is estimated against an always-on baseline.</li><li>The Pi&rsquo;s own energy use is included in the combined projection.</li><li>Direct metering is the planned accuracy upgrade.</li></ul></aside>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>The live control panels show how power decisions are made.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Current combined-load and modeled workload-attribution panels</li><li>Seven-day actual-versus-avoided energy ledger</li><li>Observed sleep history and 30-day/annual projections</li><li>Hermes cache health, job list, wake dispatch, and protection windows</li><li>AI runtime reconciliation after wake and before suspend</li><li>Subsystem eligibility matrix with explicit blockers and advisory schedule intelligence</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure class=\"screenshot\"><button data-caption=\"Power command center &mdash; Current status, controls, modeled allocation, active blockers, and Hermes schedule cache.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Power command center\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/screenshots/pfc-power-management-top.png\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Power command center</strong>Current status, controls, modeled allocation, active blockers, and Hermes schedule cache.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"screenshot\"><button data-caption=\"Energy ledger and projections &mdash; Observed online/sleep timeline, seven-day avoided energy, and current long-term projection cards.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Energy ledger and projections\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/screenshots/pfc-energy-ledger.png\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Energy ledger and projections</strong>Observed online/sleep timeline, seven-day avoided energy, and current long-term projection cards.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"screenshot\"><button data-caption=\"AI runtime orchestration &mdash; Adopted llama.cpp runtimes, endpoint identity, telemetry, and wake/GPU reconciliation.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"AI runtime orchestration\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/screenshots/pfc-ai-runtime.png\"/></button><figcaption><strong>AI runtime orchestration</strong>Adopted llama.cpp runtimes, endpoint identity, telemetry, and wake/GPU reconciliation.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"screenshot\"><button data-caption=\"Suspend eligibility matrix &mdash; The safety engine explains which subsystem allows or blocks automatic suspend.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Suspend eligibility matrix\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/screenshots/pfc-eligibility-matrix.png\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Suspend eligibility matrix</strong>The safety engine explains which subsystem allows or blocks automatic suspend.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>Lower idle power without giving up schedules or recovery.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Useful sleep instead of blind shutdown</h3><p>The server can stay off during genuine idle time without giving up scheduled jobs or operator visibility.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Power savings with context</h3><p>The dashboard ties savings to observed sleep and configured baselines, while keeping estimation limits visible.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Safer automation</h3><p>A blocked suspend is treated as a successful safety decision, not a failure to automate.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The highest-value next step is direct metering for the PFP host. That will replace more modeled attribution with measured wall-power data and make the savings ledger more defensible over longer periods.","fallbackPage":"projects/pfc-supervisor/"},"ai-server":{"id":"ai-server","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Local AI infrastructure","title":"B70 local AI server","lede":"A custom local-AI platform that I use as an engineering lab: private inference, model benchmarking, role-specific evaluation, application hosting, and the compute backbone for Print Orchestrator and PFC.","status":["Active platform","Local inference","Benchmark-driven","Private network"],"image":"assets/images/ai-img-8931.jpg","imageAlt":"Custom orange and black B70 local AI server with integrated front display","imageCaption":"Custom B70 server enclosure with an operator-facing display and serviceable internal layout.","metrics":[{"value":"72.91 tok/s","label":"Qwen3.6-35B-A3B Q4 generation result"},{"value":"93.31 tok/s","label":"Qwen3-30B-A3B Q4 generation result"},{"value":"20.61 GiB","label":"Qwen3.6 model size in the recorded test"},{"value":"0 cloud dependency","label":"for normal local inference workflows"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>I built the B70 around the local AI work I actually wanted to run.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>I wanted a private system that could run useful models locally, support long contexts, host engineering tools, and remain under my control. A benchmark-only build was not enough; the machine needed to become dependable infrastructure.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">System</p><h3>What I built</h3><p>The B70 is a custom server, model host, application platform, and test bench. It runs llama.cpp services, stores model configurations, exposes local endpoints, hosts dashboards, and supports the printer and power-control projects.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Method</p><h3>How I evaluate models</h3><p>I compare models by role instead of using one general score. Manager tests cover scope discipline, delegation, recovery, preservation, and verification. Worker tests cover coding, shell/tool use, implementation accuracy, debugging, and throughput.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Iteration</p><h3>What changed over time</h3><p>I retired the 122B model after proving that it could run because it was too large and impractical for the hardware. That decision is part of the engineering evidence: the best configuration is the one that remains useful in the real workflow.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>A useful local model is more than a single speed number.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Benchmark on the actual machine</h3><p>Generation and prompt-processing measurements come from the B70 configuration rather than internet averages or vendor specifications.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Separate manager and worker roles</h3><p>A model that plans well may not be the best model for shell work or coding. I test replacement potential against the job it would actually perform.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Keep launch settings reproducible</h3><p>Model recommendations include the full context, GPU-layer, cache, and server launch settings so a result can be repeated.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Retire impractical configurations</h3><p>The 122B stress test remains documented, but it is excluded from the active stack because the cost in memory and usability was not justified.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"benchmarks","label":"Benchmarks","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Benchmarks</p><h2>What the B70 can run in practice.</h2></div><p>Results are tied to the actual hardware, model format, and launch configuration.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-special-table-wrap\"><table class=\"project-special-table\"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Class</th><th>Recorded size</th><th>Generation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"\"><td><strong>qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-q4_k_m</strong></td><td>Dense tiny</td><td>0.46 GiB</td><td>223.42 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>gemma-4-E2B-it-UD-Q4_K_XL</strong></td><td>Small Gemma</td><td>2.97 GiB</td><td>128.83 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"highlight\"><td><strong>Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507-Q4_K_M</strong></td><td>MoE</td><td>17.28 GiB</td><td>93.31 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>gemma-4-E4B-it-UD-Q4_K_XL</strong></td><td>Small Gemma</td><td>4.77 GiB</td><td>87.54 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-UD-Q4_K_M</strong></td><td>MoE</td><td>15.78 GiB</td><td>73.67 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"highlight\"><td><strong>Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M</strong></td><td>MoE</td><td>20.61 GiB</td><td>72.91 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>gemma-4-12b-it-UD-Q4_K_XL</strong></td><td>Dense</td><td>6.86 GiB</td><td>47.93 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>Mistral-Small-3.2-24B-Instruct-2506-Q4_K_M</strong></td><td>Dense</td><td>13.34 GiB</td><td>32.65 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>Qwen3.5-27B-Q4_K_M</strong></td><td>Dense</td><td>15.58 GiB</td><td>24.39 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>Qwen3-32B-Q4_K_M</strong></td><td>Dense</td><td>18.40 GiB</td><td>23.30 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"\"><td><strong>gemma-4-31B-it-UD-Q4_K_XL</strong></td><td>Dense</td><td>17.52 GiB</td><td>22.36&ndash;22.82 tok/s</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"retired\"><td><strong>Qwen3.5-122B-A10B-UD-IQ3_S</strong></td><td>Retired stress test</td><td>retired</td><td>10.15&ndash;13.13 tok/s</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"project-special-note\"><strong>Historical stress test:</strong> Qwen3.5-122B-A10B-UD-IQ3_S reached about 248 tok/s prompt processing at pp512 and 10.15&ndash;13.13 tok/s generation around <code>-ngl 35</code> with 16k&ndash;24k context. I retired it from the active stack because it was too large and impractical for the hardware.</p>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Hardware, services, and benchmark evidence from the active system.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Custom enclosure and integrated status display</li><li>Local llama.cpp endpoints and systemd-managed runtimes</li><li>Generation-rate benchmark matrix across dense, MoE, and small models</li><li>Role-specific manager/worker model evaluation</li><li>PFC wake/recovery integration and Print Orchestrator application hosting</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Finished B70 system &mdash; The server is packaged as an operator-facing system rather than an open test bench.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Finished B70 system\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/ai-img-8931.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Finished B70 system</strong>The server is packaged as an operator-facing system rather than an open test bench.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Internal hardware layout &mdash; Serviceability, airflow, component placement, and wiring all matter because the machine runs as infrastructure.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Internal hardware layout\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/ai-img-8916.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Internal hardware layout</strong>Serviceability, airflow, component placement, and wiring all matter because the machine runs as infrastructure.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Applied local-AI workflow &mdash; The platform is used for real engineering and software work, not only model speed tests.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Applied local-AI workflow\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/ai-calculator-code-2.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Applied local-AI workflow</strong>The platform is used for real engineering and software work, not only model speed tests.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A private AI platform that now supports several other projects.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>A practical active stack</h3><p>Fast MoE models provide a better balance of capability, generation speed, and memory use than the retired oversized configuration.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Infrastructure for other projects</h3><p>The server now supports AI runtimes, dashboards, automation, model evaluation, and local development workflows.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>A repeatable test process</h3><p>Configurations and results can be compared over time as runtimes, models, and application roles change.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The next phase is deeper task-based evaluation against Muse and Qwen3.6, plus more automated capture of quality, recovery, and throughput across manager and worker roles.","fallbackPage":"projects/ai-server/"},"e3ng":{"id":"e3ng","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Custom motion system","title":"E3NG CoreXY conversion","lede":"An Ender 3 Pro rebuilt into a custom CoreXY platform with Klipper, networked toolhead electronics, calibration, and the kind of commissioning work that turns a collection of parts into a dependable machine.","status":["Operational machine","CoreXY","Klipper","CAN-style toolhead network"],"image":"assets/images/e3ng-img-7200-1.jpg","imageAlt":"Completed E3NG CoreXY printer conversion","imageCaption":"Completed E3NG platform after mechanical, electrical, firmware, and calibration work.","metrics":[{"value":"CoreXY","label":"custom motion conversion"},{"value":"SKR Mini E3 V3","label":"main controller"},{"value":"Klipper","label":"firmware and calibration workflow"},{"value":"Input Shaper","label":"completed motion tuning"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>I turned an Ender 3 platform into a custom CoreXY machine.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>I wanted more than a cosmetic Ender upgrade. The goal was to use the donor machine as the basis for a CoreXY platform that could support higher-speed motion, engineering materials, and deeper controls work.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">System</p><h3>What I built</h3><p>The conversion combined a new frame and belt path, custom toolhead packaging, Raspberry Pi control, SKR electronics, a U2C/CAN-style interface, sensors, camera, Klipper configuration, and a full calibration workflow.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Challenge</p><h3>The hard part</h3><p>The project crossed mechanical, electrical, Linux, firmware, and thermal boundaries. A symptom such as failed homing or bad first layers could come from alignment, wiring, network configuration, sensor behavior, or software settings.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Impact</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>The machine became a small automated workcell that I could assemble, commission, monitor, troubleshoot, and improve. Those are the same habits needed in larger manufacturing and automation systems.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Mechanical alignment, electronics, and firmware had to work as one system.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Treat alignment as a system problem</h3><p>Frame squareness, belt routing, gantry movement, bed alignment, and toolhead stiffness were checked together instead of tuned in isolation.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Move calibration into a repeatable workflow</h3><p>Input Shaper, bed mesh, homing, extrusion, thermal behavior, and repeatability checks became documented commissioning steps.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Use networked toolhead electronics carefully</h3><p>CAN-style connectivity reduced the toolhead wiring burden but added Linux network and interface failure modes that had to be understood and recovered.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Build for service access</h3><p>Controller, Pi, power, camera, probe, and toolhead connections were arranged so future diagnosis would not require dismantling the entire machine.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Build photos and calibration results from the conversion.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>CoreXY frame, gantry, and belt-path integration</li><li>SKR Mini E3 V3, Raspberry Pi, U2C/CAN-style interface, and toolhead electronics</li><li>Probe, camera, hotend, and extrusion-system integration</li><li>Klipper/Mainsail configuration, bed mesh, Input Shaper, and recovery work</li><li>Functional printing and ongoing use inside Print Orchestrator</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Completed CoreXY platform &mdash; The finished machine is the result of mechanical, electrical, and firmware commissioning.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Completed CoreXY platform\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/e3ng-img-7200-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Completed CoreXY platform</strong>The finished machine is the result of mechanical, electrical, and firmware commissioning.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Controller and network integration &mdash; Mainboard, Raspberry Pi, and interface hardware during integration.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Controller and network integration\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/e3ng-img-7128-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Controller and network integration</strong>Mainboard, Raspberry Pi, and interface hardware during integration.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Build and calibration evidence &mdash; Physical access and wiring remain important after the first successful motion test.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Build and calibration evidence\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/e3ng-img-7150-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Build and calibration evidence</strong>Physical access and wiring remain important after the first successful motion test.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A working motion platform and deeper controls troubleshooting experience.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Working motion platform</h3><p>The donor printer became a functional CoreXY machine rather than a partial conversion.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Controls troubleshooting experience</h3><p>The project created practical experience with sensors, motion, networked electronics, Linux, and firmware recovery.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>A test bed for software</h3><p>E3NG is now one of the real machines represented in Print Orchestrator and the wider lab environment.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"Ongoing work centers on reliability, toolhead refinement, materials, and using fleet data to make maintenance and recovery more systematic.","fallbackPage":"projects/e3ng/"},"voron-systems":{"id":"voron-systems","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Rapid prototyping systems","title":"Voron printer systems","lede":"Large-format enclosed CoreXY printers treated as automated workcells: precise assembly, thermal management, toolhead integration, calibration, maintenance, and repeatable production with technical materials.","status":["Operational printer","Enclosed CoreXY","Technical materials","Ongoing maintenance"],"image":"assets/images/voron-img-6388-1.jpg","imageAlt":"Voron toolhead and hotend components during machine integration","imageCaption":"Toolhead and hotend integration work from the Voron system.","metrics":[{"value":"Voron 2.4","label":"primary enclosed platform"},{"value":"CoreXY","label":"motion architecture"},{"value":"ASA","label":"repeatable technical-material workflow"},{"value":"Commissioning","label":"build, tune, run, maintain"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>I build and maintain enclosed printers as small production systems.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>Functional parts and engineering materials need more than a hobby printer that works occasionally. I wanted an enclosed, serviceable platform that could hold alignment, manage chamber heat, and recover predictably from faults.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">System</p><h3>What I built and maintain</h3><p>The Voron work covers frame assembly, gantry and belt setup, independent Z alignment, electronics bays, toolheads, probes, cameras, hotends, enclosure behavior, profiles, and production use.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Troubleshooting</p><h3>How I approach failures</h3><p>I separate mechanical drag, belt errors, thermal instability, wiring, firmware, slicing, and material issues instead of changing several variables at once. That keeps recovery evidence useful.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Relevance</p><h3>Why it belongs in the portfolio</h3><p>These printers show the full equipment-support loop: assemble, inspect, energize, test, tune, document, run, and maintain.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Reliability came from geometry, thermal control, and repeatable maintenance.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Prioritize geometric accuracy before speed</h3><p>Frame squareness, gantry movement, belt tension, Z alignment, and probe repeatability set the ceiling for every later tuning step.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Treat the enclosure as part of the process</h3><p>Chamber temperature, electronics cooling, material behavior, and service access have to coexist.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Standardize profiles and maintenance</h3><p>Reliable material profiles and repeatable checks matter more than a single fast showcase print.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Document ambiguous failures</h3><p>When multiple subsystems can produce the same symptom, the notes and evidence from prior failures shorten future diagnosis.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Build, wiring, and production evidence from the printer fleet.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Frame and motion-system assembly</li><li>Toolhead, hotend, probe, and camera integration</li><li>Electronics-bay organization and thermal management</li><li>ASA and functional-part production</li><li>Calibration, maintenance, and fault isolation</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Toolhead system integration &mdash; Hotend, carriage, probe, cooling, and wiring have to work as one serviceable package.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Toolhead system integration\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/voron-img-6388-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Toolhead system integration</strong>Hotend, carriage, probe, cooling, and wiring have to work as one serviceable package.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Shared commissioning discipline &mdash; The same build-test-tune-maintain mindset carries across the custom printer fleet.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Shared commissioning discipline\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/e3ng-img-7150-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Shared commissioning discipline</strong>The same build-test-tune-maintain mindset carries across the custom printer fleet.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>Reliable prototyping capacity for technical materials and functional parts.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Reliable prototyping capacity</h3><p>The system supports functional parts and larger-format work with technical materials.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Equipment-support mindset</h3><p>The project reinforces calibration, maintenance, repeatability, and operator-style troubleshooting.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Cross-project value</h3><p>The Voron platform supplies parts and operational data for other engineering projects and Print Orchestrator.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The next improvements are centered on profile validation, maintenance history, and using fleet telemetry to catch drift before it becomes a failed print.","fallbackPage":"projects/voron-systems/"},"metuned":{"id":"metuned","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Motorsports product development","title":"Metuned digital dash","lede":"A race-electronics concept that started with a real endurance-racing instrumentation problem and became a product-focused enclosure, packaging, cooling, branding, and prototype effort.","status":["Co-founder / CDO","Prototype hardware","Motorsports use case","Product development"],"image":"assets/images/metuned-copy-of-proto-2.jpg","imageAlt":"Metuned digital dashboard enclosure prototype","imageCaption":"Printed enclosure prototype used to work through electronics fit, controls, cooling, and product presentation.","metrics":[{"value":"1","label":"race problem translated into a product concept"},{"value":"PCB + display","label":"packaged inside a serviceable enclosure"},{"value":"3D printed","label":"rapid enclosure iterations"},{"value":"Track-informed","label":"requirements and design decisions"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>A race-day information problem became a product idea.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>During endurance racing, the factory instrumentation did not give the driver enough information to diagnose developing problems. Poor visibility contributed to misdiagnosis, downtime, and expensive recovery.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Project goal</p><h3>What we set out to build</h3><p>Metuned is an affordable, customizable digital dashboard concept for race and enthusiast cars. The product has to package electronics, remain visible to the driver, handle cooling and wiring, and still be serviceable.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Ownership</p><h3>My role</h3><p>As co-founder and Chief Design Officer, I focused on the enclosure, PCB and display fit, physical controls, cooling, mounting, branding, prototype narrative, and connecting the race-team problem to a product that could be explained to sponsors and customers.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Constraint</p><h3>The design constraint</h3><p>A good-looking enclosure is not enough. It has to survive vibration and heat, leave room for connectors and airflow, allow assembly, and make the screen and controls usable in a vehicle.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Packaging, cooling, service access, and the driver experience shaped the design.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Start from the driver&rsquo;s problem</h3><p>The design is driven by what the driver needs to see and operate, not by what is easiest to place on a PCB.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Prototype packaging early</h3><p>Printed enclosures make it possible to test fit, connector access, fan placement, button spacing, and serviceability before committing to a final process.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Use branding to support clarity</h3><p>The visual identity, panel graphics, and race aesthetic are part of making a technical product understandable and credible.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Keep manufacturing in the conversation</h3><p>Wall thickness, fastening, airflow, tolerances, assembly sequence, and future sheet/plate options influence the CAD from the start.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>CAD, enclosure prototypes, electronics fitment, and race context.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Enclosure and panel CAD</li><li>PCB, display, fan, connector, and control fitment</li><li>Printed prototype iterations</li><li>Branding and product communication</li><li>Requirements informed by motorsports use</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Prototype enclosure &mdash; Physical fit checks reveal packaging issues that are easy to miss in CAD.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Prototype enclosure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/metuned-copy-of-proto-2.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Prototype enclosure</strong>Physical fit checks reveal packaging issues that are easy to miss in CAD.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Dashboard packaging and presentation &mdash; The prototype connects electronics, enclosure, controls, and product identity.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Dashboard packaging and presentation\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/metuned-copy-of-proto-4-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Dashboard packaging and presentation</strong>The prototype connects electronics, enclosure, controls, and product identity.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Real motorsports context &mdash; The product grew from problems encountered around the student race program.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Real motorsports context\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/motorsports-img-5023-2.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Real motorsports context</strong>The product grew from problems encountered around the student race program.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A stronger path from field problem to manufacturable product.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Problem-to-product thinking</h3><p>A race-team failure mode became a structured product-development problem.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Rapid physical iteration</h3><p>Printed prototypes made packaging and serviceability decisions visible early.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Stronger technical storytelling</h3><p>The project can be explained to drivers, sponsors, teammates, and potential customers without losing the engineering details.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The next major step is a tighter production-intent enclosure and a clearer validation plan for heat, vibration, visibility, connector retention, and installation.","fallbackPage":"projects/metuned/"},"motorsports":{"id":"motorsports","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Team leadership and fabrication","title":"Sierra College Motorsports","lede":"A student race program built through leadership, community events, sponsorship, mechanical work, fabrication, and the practical pressure of getting a low-cost Mini Cooper ready to compete.","status":["Treasurer → VP → President","Race preparation","Community events","Hands-on repair"],"image":"assets/images/motorsports-thebigpicture-1.jpg","imageAlt":"Large Sierra College Cars and Coffee event","imageCaption":"Cars & Coffee became part of the club’s visibility, community, recruiting, and funding pipeline.","metrics":[{"value":"$500","label":"starting Mini Cooper project"},{"value":"3 roles","label":"treasurer, vice president, president"},{"value":"Team-built","label":"repairs, brackets, panels, and race prep"},{"value":"Community-funded","label":"events and sponsor outreach"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>We built a race program around a $500 Mini Cooper.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>A student race team has to solve technical problems while also finding people, money, parts, workspace, and time. The car cannot move forward if the organization around it is not working.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Team build</p><h3>What we built</h3><p>The club developed the &ldquo;Notta Miata&rdquo; Mini Cooper as a race project while creating Cars &amp; Coffee events, sponsorship relationships, recruitment, and a team structure capable of supporting the work.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Ownership</p><h3>My role</h3><p>I progressed from treasurer to vice president to president. I helped organize planning, funding, recruitment, events, race preparation, fabrication tasks, repairs, and trackside execution across a mixed-experience student team.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Engineering scope</p><h3>What made it engineering</h3><p>The work forced decisions around packaging, service access, fasteners, lifting, vibration, thermal exposure, broken components, deadlines, and safety. A solution only counted if the car could be safely assembled and run.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>The car and the organization had to improve at the same time.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Break ambiguous failures into physical tasks</h3><p>A broken race car is too large a problem to hand to one person. We separated diagnosis, parts, fabrication, access, reassembly, and inspection into work the team could execute.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Build the organization with the car</h3><p>Events, recruiting, sponsors, and budgeting were treated as project infrastructure rather than side activities.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Use fast fixes without ignoring safety</h3><p>Deadline pressure is real, but lifting, fastening, welding, fluid, heat, and rotating systems still need safe inspection before the car returns to service.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Create learning opportunities</h3><p>The club had students with different experience levels, so the work had to be assignable, explainable, and inspectable.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Repairs, fabrication, events, and trackside work from the program.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Mini Cooper teardown, engine-system work, and race preparation</li><li>Measuring, cutting, welding support, brackets, panels, and mounts</li><li>Trackside troubleshooting and deadline-driven repair</li><li>Cars &amp; Coffee event organization</li><li>Sponsorship, budgeting, recruitment, and team leadership</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Community and funding pipeline &mdash; The event program gave the club visibility, recruiting reach, and a path to self-funding.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Community and funding pipeline\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/motorsports-thebigpicture-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Community and funding pipeline</strong>The event program gave the club visibility, recruiting reach, and a path to self-funding.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Notta Miata race project &mdash; The Mini Cooper work required practical diagnosis, repair, fabrication, and team coordination.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Notta Miata race project\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/motorsports-img-5023-2.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Notta Miata race project</strong>The Mini Cooper work required practical diagnosis, repair, fabrication, and team coordination.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Race experience becoming a product &mdash; The same motorsports context helped shape the Metuned digital-dash concept.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Race experience becoming a product\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/metuned-copy-of-proto-4-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Race experience becoming a product</strong>The same motorsports context helped shape the Metuned digital-dash concept.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A practical lesson in leadership, safety, and engineering under pressure.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Leadership under constraints</h3><p>The project required decisions with limited money, time, parts, and mixed team experience.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Practical fabrication judgment</h3><p>Repairs had to account for fit, access, strength, serviceability, and safety.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>A lasting project pipeline</h3><p>Events and sponsor relationships made the technical work more sustainable and visible.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The best next step for this portfolio section is to keep adding clear before/after evidence and short technical notes for the strongest repairs and fabrication decisions.","fallbackPage":"projects/motorsports/"},"espresso-machine":{"id":"espresso-machine","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"ME190 senior design","title":"Hybrid hand-actuated espresso machine","lede":"A compact ME190 senior project that combines manual force generation with heating, sensing, pumping, controls, and a pressure-and-volume target grounded in espresso performance.","status":["ME190 senior project","$750 SendCutSend sponsorship","Prototype + drawings","Active development"],"image":"assets/images/espresso-img-4665.jpg","imageAlt":"ME190 espresso team presenting the prototype and engineering poster","imageCaption":"Senior-design presentation with the prototype and project poster.","metrics":[{"value":"9 bar","label":"target extraction pressure"},{"value":"2 oz ±10%","label":"target double-shot volume"},{"value":"25–30 s","label":"target pull time"},{"value":"$750 + merch","label":"SendCutSend sponsorship"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>Our senior design team is building a compact hybrid espresso machine.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>The team set out to build a compact machine that could produce a double shot near industry pressure, volume, and time targets while using a hand-actuated mechanism and an integrated thermal/control system.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">System</p><h3>What the system includes</h3><p>The design combines the manual mechanism, boiler or heating elements, pump support, sensors, controls, electrical power, frame, fluid path, and an enclosure within an approximately 1 &times; 2 &times; 2 ft envelope.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Contribution</p><h3>My contribution</h3><p>My work has focused on electrical fundamentals, wattage and efficiency constraints, controls and PID planning, component integration, documentation, and helping the mechanical and electrical decisions remain consistent.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Milestone</p><h3>The new milestone</h3><p>The team recently received a SendCutSend sponsorship worth $750 in store credit plus merchandise. That support gives the project more manufacturing capacity and is meaningful outside validation of the team&rsquo;s work.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Pressure, heat, timing, fabrication, and serviceability affect the same shot.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Treat pressure, heat, and timing as one system</h3><p>Extraction pressure is not useful without stable temperature, suitable flow, shot volume, and safe control behavior.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Keep manual input and powered systems coordinated</h3><p>The hand mechanism, pump, heater, sensors, and controls must complement each other instead of creating conflicting operating states.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Design around manufacturing evidence</h3><p>The drawing package, BOM, poster, test plan, and sponsored fabrication all support the transition from concept to a buildable machine.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Make safety and serviceability visible</h3><p>Hot surfaces, pressure, water, electricity, access, and maintenance have to be addressed in the layout and operating procedure.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"sponsorship","label":"Sponsorship","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Sponsorship</p><h2>SendCutSend is helping us turn the design into real parts.</h2></div><p>The support adds outside validation and more room for production-intent fabrication.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-milestone\"><div class=\"project-milestone-metric\"><div><strong>$750</strong><span>SendCutSend store credit + merchandise</span></div></div><div class=\"project-milestone-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">SendCutSend sponsorship</p><h3>A meaningful step from student prototype toward production-intent parts.</h3><p>The next portfolio update should identify the sponsored parts, show the submitted geometry and material choices, and connect the finished components to assembly, testing, and performance results.</p></div></div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Design documents, prototypes, and manufacturing evidence from ME190.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Prototype and team presentation evidence</li><li>System targets for pressure, volume, time, and envelope</li><li>Electrical load and efficiency calculations</li><li>Controls/PID planning and sensor integration</li><li>Assembly drawings, detail drawings, BOM, poster, and project documentation</li><li>SendCutSend sponsorship for fabrication</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Prototype and poster presentation &mdash; The team presented the machine, system targets, and engineering work together.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Prototype and poster presentation\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/espresso-img-4665.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Prototype and poster presentation</strong>The team presented the machine, system targets, and engineering work together.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Senior-design team &mdash; The project is a cross-disciplinary team effort with mechanical, electrical, and documentation responsibilities.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Senior-design team\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/espresso-group1-standing.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Senior-design team</strong>The project is a cross-disciplinary team effort with mechanical, electrical, and documentation responsibilities.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Prototype detail &mdash; Physical evidence helps connect the calculations and drawings to the current build state.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Prototype detail\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/espresso-image-113.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Prototype detail</strong>Physical evidence helps connect the calculations and drawings to the current build state.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A stronger final build path backed by outside support.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>External validation</h3><p>The SendCutSend sponsorship adds industry support and manufacturing resources to the project.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Systems integration experience</h3><p>The project requires pressure, thermal, electrical, controls, mechanism, and documentation work to stay aligned.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>A stronger final build path</h3><p>Sponsored sheet/plate fabrication can improve the quality and credibility of the production-intent structure.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The next portfolio update should show exactly how the SendCutSend credit is used, which parts move into sponsored fabrication, and the measured pressure, temperature, volume, and shot-time results from testing.","fallbackPage":"projects/espresso-machine/"},"uei-lab":{"id":"uei-lab","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Professional test engineering","title":"UEI energy-efficiency testing","lede":"Standards-driven appliance and airflow test work involving controlled environments, instrumentation, repeatable setup, data collection, repair, and documentation.","status":["Professional lab work","CEC-related testing","HVI 916 exposure","Instrumentation"],"image":"assets/images/uei-img-8868.jpg","imageAlt":"Residential fan airflow test duct and instrumentation setup","imageCaption":"Residential fan airflow/CFM setup used for standards-aligned testing.","metrics":[{"value":"8 ft cube","label":"approximate controlled whole-house fan room"},{"value":"CFM","label":"airflow performance measurement"},{"value":"CEC","label":"appliance-efficiency context"},{"value":"Repeatability","label":"setup, repair, data, documentation"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>I support appliance and airflow testing in a standards-driven lab.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>Advertised airflow and appliance efficiency have to be checked under controlled conditions. Small leaks, sensor placement, ambient conditions, or inconsistent procedure can change the result.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Hands-on work</p><h3>What I worked on</h3><p>I helped construct and maintain an approximately 8-foot-cube room for whole-house fan testing and supported portable-spa and food-heater efficiency testing in controlled thermal environments.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Ownership</p><h3>My role</h3><p>My work included the physical test environment, sealing and repairs, tubing, pressure and flow hardware, wet/dry-bulb readings, thermocouples, data logging, test setup, and documentation.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Learning</p><h3>What the work taught me</h3><p>Good testing is not only reading an instrument. The fixture, environment, leakage, calibration, procedure, timing, and notes all affect whether the data is useful.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Reliable data starts with a controlled setup and documented repairs.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Control the environment before trusting the number</h3><p>Room sealing, duct connections, fixture condition, and ambient temperature were treated as part of the measurement system.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Repair the setup without hiding the repair</h3><p>Duct sealant, tape, wood, fasteners, and fixture repairs were documented because they can affect repeatability.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Use multiple measurements together</h3><p>Pressure, flow, temperature, and power information are more useful when the timing and setup are recorded consistently.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Write for the next test</h3><p>Clear procedures and notes reduce variation when another person repeats the setup or reviews the result.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>The test fixtures, instruments, and environments behind the measurements.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>Controlled room and test-fixture construction</li><li>Fan mounting, ducts, tubing, pressure, and airflow hardware</li><li>Wet/dry-bulb and thermocouple measurements</li><li>Data logging and result documentation</li><li>Portable-spa and food-heater efficiency-test support</li><li>Standards and lab-safety awareness</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Fan airflow test system &mdash; The large fixture and controlled room are part of the measurement, not just a backdrop.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Fan airflow test system\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/uei-img-8868.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Fan airflow test system</strong>The large fixture and controlled room are part of the measurement, not just a backdrop.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Instrumentation and data collection &mdash; Tubing, temperature, pressure, and logging hardware support the final CFM and efficiency evidence.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Instrumentation and data collection\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/uei-img-8870.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Instrumentation and data collection</strong>Tubing, temperature, pressure, and logging hardware support the final CFM and efficiency evidence.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Constructed test environment &mdash; Wood construction, sealing, maintenance, and repair were required to keep the setup usable.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Constructed test environment\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/manufacturing-img-8774.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Constructed test environment</strong>Wood construction, sealing, maintenance, and repair were required to keep the setup usable.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>Professional experience with repeatability, uncertainty, and lab documentation.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Professional testing discipline</h3><p>The work connects real standards, fixtures, instruments, data, safety, and documentation.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Better understanding of uncertainty</h3><p>A plausible number is not enough; the setup and procedure determine whether it can be trusted.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Hands-on lab support</h3><p>The role required both physical repair and careful measurement rather than separating &ldquo;build&rdquo; work from &ldquo;data&rdquo; work.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"A future update should add anonymized example plots or test-flow diagrams where permitted, while keeping proprietary data and customer results out of the public portfolio.","fallbackPage":"projects/uei-lab/"},"abb-rexroth":{"id":"abb-rexroth","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Industrial robotics coursework","title":"ABB and Rexroth robotics lab","lede":"Hands-on ME165 laboratory work with industrial robot hardware, controller platforms, emergency-stop systems, I/O, cabling, and safety-aware operating procedures.","status":["ME165 coursework","Industrial hardware","Safety-aware labs","Controls exposure"],"image":"assets/images/me165-image-111.jpg","imageAlt":"ABB industrial robot and controller platform in a Sacramento State laboratory","imageCaption":"ABB robot and industrial controller hardware used in supervised ME165 laboratory work.","metrics":[{"value":"ABB","label":"industrial robot platform"},{"value":"Rexroth","label":"controller-system exposure"},{"value":"E-stop + I/O","label":"hands-on lab context"},{"value":"Coursework","label":"accurately framed experience"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>ME165 gave me hands-on exposure to industrial robotics hardware.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>The purpose of the course work was to connect controls and automation concepts to actual industrial hardware, where faults, interlocks, controller state, cabling, and safety procedures matter.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Equipment</p><h3>What I worked with</h3><p>The lab exposed me to ABB robot hardware, Rexroth controller components, emergency-stop systems, I/O, controller interfaces, and supervised setup and operation.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Scope</p><h3>How I describe the experience</h3><p>This is academic hands-on exposure, not production ownership. I keep that distinction clear while still showing that I have worked around the physical equipment and operating discipline used in industrial automation.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Impact</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>The work gives me a more concrete foundation for automation support, commissioning, maintenance, and controls-adjacent engineering than classroom diagrams alone.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Safety state, controller state, I/O, and the physical cell all matter together.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Treat safety state as part of the control system</h3><p>E-stops, guarded operation, controller state, and supervised procedures are not separate from robot motion; they define when motion is allowed.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Observe the whole cell</h3><p>Robot arm behavior, controller, I/O, cabling, fixtures, and operator actions have to be understood together.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Document what was actually done</h3><p>The portfolio avoids claiming full programming or production responsibility that was not part of the course.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Translate coursework into support skills</h3><p>The useful takeaway is familiarity with equipment states, interfaces, faults, and safety-aware troubleshooting.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>ABB and Rexroth hardware, controls, and supervised lab setups.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>ABB robot hardware exposure</li><li>Rexroth controller-platform exposure</li><li>Emergency-stop, I/O, cabling, and supervised lab setup</li><li>Motion and controller-state observation</li><li>Safety-aware technical documentation</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Industrial robotics laboratory &mdash; The robot, controller, E-stop, and cabling make the automation context visible.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Industrial robotics laboratory\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/me165-image-111.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Industrial robotics laboratory</strong>The robot, controller, E-stop, and cabling make the automation context visible.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>A practical foundation for automation and equipment-support work.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Industrial context</h3><p>Controls concepts became tied to real equipment, interfaces, and safety states.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Accurate portfolio framing</h3><p>The page shows hands-on exposure without overstating the scope of the coursework.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Relevant foundation</h3><p>The experience supports future work in automation support, commissioning, and equipment troubleshooting.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The best next step is more direct programming, I/O integration, and fault-recovery work on industrial equipment, documented with clear lab objectives and results.","fallbackPage":"projects/abb-rexroth/"},"makerspace-manufacturing":{"id":"makerspace-manufacturing","tone":"orange","eyebrow":"Manufacturing support","title":"Makerspace and manufacturing support","lede":"Hands-on support across CAD/CAM, 3D printing, CNC routing, laser systems, woodworking, user training, shop safety, deburr, packaging, and the day-to-day work that keeps equipment useful to other people.","status":["Makerspace support","CAD/CAM","Equipment training","Production support"],"image":"assets/images/manufacturing-img-8774.jpg","imageAlt":"Constructed wood fixture in a lab or manufacturing environment","imageCaption":"Fixture construction and equipment-support work connect design intent to practical build conditions.","metrics":[{"value":"3D printing","label":"setup, profiles, and user support"},{"value":"CNC + laser","label":"machine and CAM workflows"},{"value":"Shop safety","label":"training and supervised use"},{"value":"Deburr + packaging","label":"production handoff experience"}],"tabs":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Overview</p><h2>I helped people turn designs into parts while keeping equipment available.</h2></div><p>The problem, my contribution, and the constraint that shaped the work.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-story-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"01\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Context</p><h3>The problem I was solving</h3><p>A makerspace only works when equipment is safe, available, understandable, and supported. Good machines still fail users if setup, training, files, materials, or maintenance are unclear.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"02\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Hands-on work</p><h3>What I worked on</h3><p>I supported 3D printers, CNC routers, lasers, woodworking tools, embroidery and vinyl equipment, CAD/CAM workflows, user training, and shop safety. At ASB Products I also gained experience with inventory, deburr, finishing, and packaging.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"03\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Ownership</p><h3>My role</h3><p>The work combined technical assistance, machine setup, troubleshooting, training, documentation, and practical support for people with different experience levels.</p></article>\n<article class=\"project-story-card\" data-number=\"04\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Impact</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>Manufacturing engineering includes the handoff between design and production. Fixturing, tool access, material, finishing, inspection, safety, and operator understanding all influence whether a part or process succeeds.</p></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"decisions","label":"Design decisions","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Design decisions</p><h2>Good manufacturing support is as much about setup and teaching as the machine.</h2></div><p>The tradeoffs and safeguards I used to move the project forward.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-decision-grid\">\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>01</span><div><h3>Design the setup, not just the part</h3><p>A usable file still needs material choice, fixturing, tool access, machine parameters, and a safe operating plan.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>02</span><div><h3>Teach in a way that can be repeated</h3><p>The goal of support is not to complete one job for a user; it is to help them understand the machine and succeed safely next time.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>03</span><div><h3>Protect equipment uptime</h3><p>Small maintenance and setup issues are handled before they become damaged tools, failed jobs, or unsafe work.</p></div></article>\n<article class=\"project-decision\"><span>04</span><div><h3>Respect the finishing steps</h3><p>Deburr, inspection, packaging, and inventory are part of production quality even though they happen after cutting or printing.</p></div></article>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"evidence","label":"Evidence","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Evidence</p><h2>Machines, fixtures, and production work from the shop floor.</h2></div><p>These images connect the description to work that was built, tested, or operated.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-evidence-layout\">\n<div class=\"project-evidence-copy\"><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">What to look for</p><h3>The evidence connects the description to something built, tested, or operated.</h3><ul class=\"project-evidence-list\"><li>CAD/CAM and machine setup</li><li>3D printers, CNC routers, and laser systems</li><li>Woodworking, vinyl, embroidery, and general shop support</li><li>User training and safety practices</li><li>Deburr, finishing, packaging, and inventory support</li><li>Equipment uptime and troubleshooting</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"project-gallery\">\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Fixture and environment construction &mdash; Practical construction work requires material, fastener, sealing, access, and maintenance decisions.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Fixture and environment construction\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/manufacturing-img-8774.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Fixture and environment construction</strong>Practical construction work requires material, fastener, sealing, access, and maintenance decisions.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Equipment supporting a test process &mdash; Manufacturing support and test engineering overlap in fixtures, setup, repair, and repeatability.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Equipment supporting a test process\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/uei-img-8868.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Equipment supporting a test process</strong>Manufacturing support and test engineering overlap in fixtures, setup, repair, and repeatability.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><button data-caption=\"Additive manufacturing equipment &mdash; Custom printer systems extend the same setup, maintenance, and operator-support mindset.\" data-lightbox=\"\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Additive manufacturing equipment\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/images/voron-img-6388-1.jpg\"/></button><figcaption><strong>Additive manufacturing equipment</strong>Custom printer systems extend the same setup, maintenance, and operator-support mindset.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},{"id":"outcomes","label":"Results","html":"\n<div class=\"project-section-heading\"><div><p class=\"project-eyebrow\">Results</p><h2>Broader equipment fluency and better operator-support habits.</h2></div><p>The result, the main lesson, and the next useful improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"project-outcome-grid\">\n<article><span>01 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Broader equipment fluency</h3><p>The work spans multiple machine types instead of one narrow process.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Operator support habits</h3><p>Training, documentation, safety, and clear handoff became part of the technical solution.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 &middot; Outcome</span><h3>Design-to-production perspective</h3><p>The experience connects CAD with setup, finishing, inspection, and packaging realities.</p></article>\n</div>\n"}],"next":"The next update should add a few concise case studies showing a user problem, the machine/setup decision, the finished result, and the lesson for future jobs.","fallbackPage":"projects/makerspace-manufacturing/"}}};
